1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,296
What does it take to build a bank's e-commerce acquiring system from scratch?

2
00:00:04,346 --> 00:00:09,888
This series walks through one, part by part, and this map shows where each part sits.

3
00:00:09,938 --> 00:00:15,312
On top are the three users: the merchant's server, the buyer, and the bank admin.

4
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:23,232
The system is two units, and the first, ecom, is the payments product: one modular monolith with five modules.

5
00:00:23,282 --> 00:00:29,160
Partner owns the merchants: who they are, their company documents, and whether they may sell.

6
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:32,352
Commission decides the fee on every payment.

7
00:00:32,402 --> 00:00:39,336
The e POS module knows which e POS terminal, a MID and a TID, each payment goes through.

8
00:00:39,386 --> 00:00:44,640
Payment creates every payment, debits the card by its token, and resolves the outcome.

9
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:54,096
Reconciliation compares every day's debits, reversals and refunds with what each card processing centre reports, and gives each one a match state and a reason.

10
00:00:54,146 --> 00:01:00,504
ecom never sees a card number: everything that touches one lives in the second unit, the cards unit.

11
00:01:00,554 --> 00:01:05,112
The buyer types the card into the card frame, which sits inside the payment page.

12
00:01:05,162 --> 00:01:09,552
The card vault keeps card numbers encrypted, and hands out tokens instead.

13
00:01:09,602 --> 00:01:15,024
Its BIN module knows which scheme and which bank issued a card, from its first digits.

14
00:01:15,074 --> 00:01:23,448
One adapter per card processing centre speaks that centre's protocol, for the card vault and for ecom, over mutual T L S.

15
00:01:23,498 --> 00:01:39,336
Each unit has its own infrastructure: ecom runs Postgres, Redis, Keycloak, Vault, an S3 file store, Temporal, Kafka, and monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana, and the cards unit runs its own Postgres, Vault and Keycloak.

16
00:01:39,386 --> 00:01:47,592
Part zero lays the foundations: what acquiring is, the project setup, and how a domain becomes a contract and a database.

17
00:01:47,642 --> 00:01:58,920
Part one covers the merchants: sign-up, email codes, company documents, two-admin approval, Keycloak clients, merchant users, sales points, and the shared platform libraries.

18
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:05,304
Part two holds the money rules: the commission, and the e POS terminals every debit goes through.

19
00:02:05,354 --> 00:02:11,448
Part three handles cards: the processing centre adapters, then the card vault with its BIN module.

20
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:22,224
Part four is the payment, last, because it needs everything else: from signed requests and the payment page to refunds, daily reports, disputes and reconciliation.

21
00:02:22,274 --> 00:02:28,200
Part five is how it grows up: delivery onto a bank's own servers, and the cards unit up close.

22
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,984
Next, what acquiring is, in five minutes.
